        new(
            "researchy-qg008-finite-distinguishability",
            "Finiteness Is a Boundary, Not a Consequence — and Information Is Its First Casualty",
            "ResearchY Milestone · QG_008 (V2.2 New Physics — Geometry Bridge)",
            "Must distinguishability be finite? Physics requires it — but Difference does not imply it. Finiteness is a boundary: the value 96 is derived, the finiteness is an input. And if infinite distinguishability were allowed, information breaks first: log₂(N) diverges.",
            "QG_008 asks whether finite distinguishability is a necessary consequence of Difference or a remaining boundary. Verdict: finiteness is a BOUNDARY — required for physics (finite information, well-defined normalization and measure) but NOT logically implied by Difference. COMPARE FINITE vs INFINITE: finite N (95) — normalization Σρ = 1 well-defined, count conservation clear, geometry √(−g) = ρ well-defined, information log₂(95) = 6.57 bits FINITE; infinite N — normalization requires a convergent series (not automatic; a geometric series needs |r| < 1, sum = 1/(1−r)), count conservation must be defined via a limit, geometry needs a limit measure, information log₂(N) → ∞ DIVERGES. SURVIVAL TESTS: normalization — survives only with a convergence assumption; count conservation — survives via a limit; geometry — survives with a limit structure; INFORMATION — DOES NOT SURVIVE (log₂(N) → ∞, no finite content). FIRST BREAKDOWN: INFORMATION (log₂(N) diverges immediately); normalization, geometry, and measurement are SECOND (they can be extended with limits). DETERMINATION: A) finiteness required — PARTIAL (required for information/normalization/geometry); B) finiteness emergent — NO; C) finiteness boundary — YES (the VALUE N=96 is derived via closure, D_015/D_019, but the FINITENESS itself is a separate property of the state space — Difference produces the act of distinguishing, not a count). SMALLEST PRINCIPLE forcing finiteness: the CONSISTENCY requirement that the state space admit a well-defined normalization (Σρ = 1), a finite information content (log₂ N < ∞), and a measure (√(−g) = ρ) — jointly requiring N < ∞. PROVE/REFUTE: Difference implies finite state count — REFUTED. If infinite distinguishability is allowed: FIRST breakdown information, then normalization/geometry/measurement. COUNTEREXAMPLES: 'Difference implies finiteness' fails (produces distinguishability, not a count); 'infinite distinguishability has finite information' fails (log₂(N) diverges); 'normalization is automatic for infinite N' fails (needs convergence); 'geometry extends to infinite N' fails (limit measure needed). FALSIFICATION: a derivation of finiteness from Difference alone; an infinite state space with finite information; a different closure value. Classification: finiteness BOUNDARY (a property of the state space, required for physics); the value N=96 DERIVED (closure); normalization/count conservation DERIVED (from finiteness, QG_007); information DERIVED (finite only); geometry DERIVED (needs the finite measure). The chain Difference → Distinguishability → Finite State Space [BOUNDARY] → Normalization → Count Conservation → ρ → {Geometry, Information} is now complete, with finiteness as its one remaining input. No new primitive; canonical AT unchanged.",
            "For non-experts: the theory's chain of reasoning runs from 'things can be told apart' through 'the counts add up to one' to geometry and information. This audit asks about the middle: must the list of distinguishable things be FINITE? The answer is subtle and honest. Physics as the theory defines it requires finiteness — with an infinite list, the amount of information becomes infinite (the log of infinity), the 'adds to one' needs extra conditions, and the geometry needs limit machinery. So finiteness is required FOR the physics. But does the theory's first idea FORCE finiteness? No. The ability to tell things apart does not by itself decide how many things there are. The theory derives the number (96) from its closure conditions, but the fact that the list is finite at all is an input — a boundary, not a consequence. And the first thing that would break with an infinite list is information: the log of infinity is not a number.",
            "Finiteness is required for physics but not implied by Difference — and information is the first casualty of the infinite.",
            "“The list must end — not because Difference says so, but because physics needs it to.”",
            true,
            [
                new("ResearchY-QG_007", "COUNT CONSERVATION NECESSITY AUDIT",
                    "Count conservation follows from Difference via finiteness — QG_008 asks why finiteness itself.",
                    "https://github.com/MagusDraconis/AT/blob/feature/v2.2-new-physics/Docs/ResearchY/QG_GeometryBridge/ResearchY-QG_007.md"),
                new("ResearchY-QG_006", "COUNT CONSERVATION ORIGIN AUDIT",
                    "The definitional foundation that requires a finite, normalized count.",
                    "https://github.com/MagusDraconis/AT/blob/feature/v2.2-new-physics/Docs/ResearchY/QG_GeometryBridge/ResearchY-QG_006.md"),
                new("ResearchY-D_019", "CLOSURE-ONLY AUDIT",
                    "The value N=96 is derived by closure — the finiteness itself is the boundary.",
                    "https://github.com/MagusDraconis/AT/blob/feature/v2.2-new-physics/Docs/ResearchY/D_ResonanceStructure/ResearchY-D_019.md"),
                new("ResearchY-QG_008", "FINITE DISTINGUISHABILITY AUDIT",
                    "Finiteness is BOUNDARY; information is the first breakdown at N → ∞.",
                    "https://github.com/MagusDraconis/AT/blob/feature/v2.2-new-physics/Docs/ResearchY/QG_GeometryBridge/ResearchY-QG_008.md"),
